      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total Number of Arrangements:**\n   Since the table is round, the total number of distinct ways to arrange 9 people is given by the factorial of 9:\n   \\[\n   9! = 362880\n   \\]\n\n2. **Arrangements with Math Majors Consecutive:**\n   First, consider that the 4 math majors can be treated as a single \"block\" or \"super person.\" This blocks 4 people. We choose 1 of the 4 physics majors and 1 of the 3 biology majors to fill the remaining 4 seats. There are \\(4!\\) ways to arrange the math majors within their block. The remaining positions must then be one physics and one biology major.\n\n   - Choose 1 of the 3 biology majors to fill the remaining seat with the physics major: \n     \\[\n     3! = 6\n     \\]\n   - Arrange the chosen 4 people in the block and the remaining 4 people:\n     \\[\n     4! \\times 4! = 24 \\times 24 = 576\n     \\]\n\n   For each choice of the 4 math majors sitting consecutively, there are 4! (4 factorial) ways to arrange the set of 4 math majors, and 3! ways to arrange the remaining 3 people.\n\n   Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements where the math majors are together is:\n   \\[\n   4! \\times 4! \\times 6 \\times 24\n   \\]\n\n3. **Probability Calculation:**\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{all 4 math majors sit together}) = \\frac{4! \\times 4! \\times 6 \\times 24}{9!}\n   \\]\n\n   First, simplify the calculations:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{24 \\times 24 \\times 6}{362880}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3456}{362880}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\frac{1}{105}}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit together in consecutive seats is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{105}}\\).",
