      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats when nine people sit down at a round table. We will use complementary counting to simplify our calculations.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to arrange nine people around a round table. Since the table is round, we fix one person in one seat to avoid counting rotations as different arrangements. This leaves us with \\(8!\\) ways to arrange the remaining eight people. So, the total number of arrangements is:\n\\[\n8! = 40320\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to arrange the nine people such that the four math majors are not all consecutive. We first count the number of ways to arrange the nine people without any restrictions, and then subtract the number of ways in which the four math majors are all consecutive.\n\n1. **Total number of arrangements**: As calculated above, this is \\(40320\\).\n\n2. **Number of arrangements where all four math majors are not consecutive**: We use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. First, we count the number of ways to arrange the nine people such that all four math majors are not in consecutive seats. We treat the block of 4 math majors as a single \"super person\". This reduces the problem to arranging 6 items (5 physics majors, 1 biology person, and 1 \"super person\" of math majors) around a round table. Fixing one of the physics majors gives \\(5!\\) ways to arrange the remaining 5 physics majors and the biology person, and within the \"super person\" block, the 4 math majors can be arranged in \\(4!\\) ways. So, the number of arrangements where all four math majors are not consecutive is:\n\\[\n6! \\times 4! = 720 \\times 24 = 17280\n\\]\n\n3. **Probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats**: The complement of the event that all four math majors are not consecutive is the event that they are all consecutive. Therefore, the number of favorable outcomes where all four math majors are consecutive is:\n\\[\n40320 - 17280 = 23040\n\\]\n\n4. **Probability**: The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of arrangements:\n\\[\n\\frac{23040}{40320} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
